  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000124363,
      "SummaryText": "Accidental release preventuion and emergency response policies\n\nDreyer's Grand Ice Cream tests, inspects and maintains ammonia equipment at its facility as part of a mechanical integrity program based on  the engineering practices prescribed in IIAR-6 2019. \nThe Dreyer\u0092s Grand Ice Cream facility has an emergency action plan (Plan) in effect. The Plan was recently updated, and will be reviewed and updated in the future as needed.  The Plan was designed to meet the following objectives:\n1.) To save lives.\n2.) To minimize and avoid injuries.\n3.) To protect the environment.\n4.) To minimize property damage.\n\nThe Plan identifies those responsible for responding to an emergency and addresses notification procedures, evacuation routes, ammonia health hazards, and mitigation procedures which will be implemented to respond effectively to emergency situations that may arise at the facility.   Dreyer\u0092s Grand Ice Cream facility is a responding site with a local HAZMAT team that is trained to respond to ammonia related emergencies. In addition employees are trained to evacuate in case of ammonia and fire emergencies via annual evacuation drills. \n\n\nThe Plan provides for Dreyer\u0092s Grand Ice Cream\u0092s emergency response efforts to be coordinated with the local fire department. In the case of an ammonia-related emergency, it is the policy of Dreyer\u0092s Grand Ice Cream to evacuate and to allow the fire department to respond to the emergency. \n\n\nThe stationary source and regulated substances\n\nThe facility at Dreyer\u0092s Grand Ice Cream was constructed in 2005 and has been periodically expanded since that time. It serves as a key manufacturing and distribution center for a wide range of frozen dessert products. The ammonia system at the facility was constructed in accordance with applicable federal, state and local regulations including the Uniform Fire and Mechanical Codes.\n\n The system provides refrigeration for evaporators, freezers, manufacturing lines, and penthouses. This system consists"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000124363,
      "SummaryText": "of high-stage screw compressors, swing screw compressors, ammonia pumps, evaporative condensers, penthouse evaporators (serving both docks and warehouse), oil pots, oil stills, accumulators, recirculators, ice cream hardeners, tunnels, freezers, surge drums, and heat exchangers.\n\nGeneral Accidential Release Prevention Program\n\nThe facility has integrated various engineering and administrative control mechanisms to eliminate and/or minimize any possible upsets or releases.  These control mechanisms includie; electronic ammonia detection  and alarms that are located throughout the facility, safety devices and interlocks that will shut down equipment when safe operating limits are exceeded, safety relief valves are installed on pressure vessels and oil separators to reduce the  possibility of an over-pressurization condition.\n\nFive-year accident history\nThere have been no ammonia accidents at the facility, including any reportable releases during the previous 5 year period (since 2020).\n\nEmergency Response Program\n\nThe facility\u0092s n emergency action plan and program is described under Accidental Release and Emergency Response Procedures.  \n\nPlanned Changes to Improve Safety\n\nThe facility completed a compliance audit, 5-yr PHA revalidation, a 5-yr Mechanical Integrity audit, and IIAR-9 gap analysis in 2025. The recommendations from these audits and the IIAR-9 gap analysis are being tracked and either have been addressed or are in the process of being addressed recommendation"
    }
  ]
